def _storyboard(
    slides: list[SlideSpec], run_dir: Path, asset_rows: list[dict[str, Any]]
) -> Path:
    by_slide: dict[int, list[str]] = {}
    for row in asset_rows:
        by_slide.setdefault(int(row["slide_number"]), []).append(
            str(row["exact_asset_path"])
        )
    lines = [
        "# PHM RT-DETR professor update — presentation storyboard",
        "",
        "> Scope: canonical R3 frozen-encoder scalar-regression milestone only. Later R4 pseudo-box, detector and multitask work is intentionally excluded.",
        "",
    ]
    for slide in slides:
        lines += [
            f"## Slide {slide.number} — {slide.title}",
            "",
            f"**Section:** {slide.section}",
            "",
            f"**Purpose:** {slide.purpose}",
            "",
            "**Exact on-slide wording:**",
            "",
            *[f"- {item}" for item in slide.on_slide],
            "",
            "**Exact figure/table path(s):**",
            "",
            *[f"- `{path}`" for path in by_slide.get(slide.number, [])],
            "",
            f"**Layout:** {slide.layout}",
            "",
            f"**Speaker notes (45–75 seconds):** {slide.notes}",
            "",
            f"**Professor question answered:** {slide.professor_question}",
            "",
            f"**Scientific warning:** {slide.warning}",
            "",
        ]
    path = run_dir / "PRESENTATION_STORYBOARD.md"
    path.write_text("\n".join(lines), encoding="utf-8")
    return path


def _professor_qa(run_dir: Path) -> Path:
    text = """# Professor Q&A — canonical R3 image baseline

> All targets and performance values are provisional pending human review. They are not organizer ground truth or calibrated physical spall measurements.

## What was the model input?

One post-run gear-tooth JPEG view, originally `1440×2560×3`. Ultralytics scale-filled it to `640×640`, converted BGR to RGB/BCHW `float32`, and divided by 255.

## What was the prediction target?

`per_image_damage_candidate_pct`: the percentage of valid visible-flank ROI pixels selected by the versioned provisional candidate mask.

## Was the target official ground truth?

No. It is a self-defined, image-derived pseudo-target required for an engineering baseline. The organizer did not supply participant training labels, and all 560 tooth/run records remain pending human review.

## How was the target generated?

A fixed visible-flank band was illumination-normalized; elongated dark/horizontal components were selected. Candidate pixels divided by valid ROI pixels, multiplied by 100, produced one image value. Multiple views used a maximum to form a tooth value; the three largest tooth values were averaged for the raw run target. A cumulative maximum formed the optional causal monotonic trajectory.

## What does six hours mean?

It is the typical operational-run, post-run inspection, and desired output cadence. It is not automatically a six-hour-ahead forecast horizon and this baseline is not remaining-useful-life prediction.

## Was the task regression or detection?

Regression. The canonical model outputs one scalar per image view. Standard RT-DETR detection was not scientifically valid at this milestone because no authoritative damage boxes/classes existed.

## Why use RT-DETR?

To test whether its COCO-pretrained multiscale image representation transfers to the gear-tooth pseudo-target. The experiment evaluates representation transfer, not standard detection.

## What was frozen and what was trained?

The 32,148,140-parameter RT-DETR-L encoder was frozen. Three pooled feature scales formed a 768-vector. Only a 98,689-parameter `768→128→1` regression head was trained.

## What was the train/validation/test split?

- Train: EXP-B, 448 image views.
- Validation: EXP-A, 323 image views.
- Test: EXP-F, 224 untouched image views.

## Was the split random?

No. It was a deterministic strict experiment-level split. Related views, teeth, runs and near-duplicate groups did not cross partitions. The saved validation report records zero experiment/run and near-duplicate crossings.

## What was the prediction compared against?

At image-view level, each predicted scalar was compared with that same view's `per_image_damage_candidate_pct`. Tooth and run metrics were calculated only after applying the same maximum-view and top-three aggregation rules to truth and predictions.

## How were MAE and RMSE calculated?

For matching records at one aggregation level, `MAE = mean(|prediction − pseudo-target|)` and `RMSE = sqrt(mean((prediction − pseudo-target)²))`. Units are provisional percentage points of visible-flank candidate area.

## Why is R² negative?

Negative R² means the model's squared prediction error exceeds the error of a constant test-mean predictor. It signals poor absolute-value calibration and weak cross-protocol generalization.

## Is Spearman 0.935 a strong result?

Not by itself. It occurs after cumulative-maximum smoothing on only eight EXP-F runs. At that same level MAE is 1.920 pp, RMSE 2.203 pp and R² −5.422. It suggests ordered trend retention, not accurate values.

## What does acquisition-protocol shift mean?

EXP-A/B use ten camera-sequence close-ups for teeth 1–4, whereas EXP-F primarily uses one canonical image per tooth. The framing and view multiplicity differ, so test error reflects both unseen-experiment and image-protocol differences.

## Can the result be used as a physical damage measurement?

No. The mask may select shadows, texture or edges; the target is uncalibrated; all tooth values require human review; and cross-protocol calibration is weak.

## What is required before PatchTST and fusion?

Review the 560 tooth records, approve or revise the ROI/mask, decide the maximum-view and top-three aggregation rules, assess A/B-versus-F comparability, then freeze a new or accepted target version. Only afterward should bounded sensor features and PatchTST begin. Fusion should wait until both unimodal baselines are independently valid.
"""
    path = run_dir / "PROFESSOR_QA.md"
    path.write_text(text, encoding="utf-8")
    return path


